const ELEC275_CHAPTER_NOTES={
1:[
noteSection('networks','1.1 · NETWORKS & CIRCUITS','See the elements sitting between the nodes.','A network is any collection of interconnected elements; a circuit is a network with at least one closed path around which charge can flow. Elements are joined by ideal wires, which lose no voltage, so every point on a connected wire region — a node — sits at the same potential. A branch is a single pathway carrying one current, a loop is any closed path, and a mesh is a loop that contains no smaller loop. Elements on one branch are in series; elements between the same two nodes are in parallel.','Treat a circuit diagram as a subway map. The stations are nodes and the track segments between them are branches; stretching or redrawing a line changes nothing as long as the same stations stay connected. This is only an analogy: trains are not charge, but the connectivity logic is exactly the same.',String.raw`\text{one node}\Rightarrow\text{one voltage},\qquad \text{one branch}\Rightarrow\text{one current}`,['An ideal wire drops no voltage, so a node can stretch across a whole diagram and still be one electrical point.','A junction is just a meeting of wires; a node may contain several junctions, or none at all.','Elements in series share one current because they sit on the same unbranched path; elements in parallel share one voltage because they sit between the same two nodes.','There can be only one reference node in a network, even when its symbol is drawn several times to reduce wiring clutter.'],{title:'Count the nodes in four networks (Example 1.2)',steps:[['Mark every ideal-wire region',String.raw`\text{a node}=\text{all points joined by ideal wire only}`,'Crossing an element ends the node; plain wire never does.'],['Figure 1.9: one ring of four elements',String.raw`4\ \text{elements in a loop}\Rightarrow 4\ \text{nodes}`,'There is one node between each pair of neighbouring elements.'],['Figure 1.10: two rails and a middle tap',String.raw`\text{top rail}+\text{bottom rail}+\text{middle node}=3\ \text{nodes}`,'The source and the 2K and 6K resistors share the two large rails; a third node sits between the two 3K resistors.'],['Figures 1.11 and 1.12',String.raw`5\ \text{nodes (op-amp)},\qquad 2\ \text{nodes (headlamps)}`,'The op-amp network has five nodes; the 12-V battery with two headlamps has one node on each battery terminal.']],answer:'Four, three, five, and two nodes. The headlamp circuit (Figure 1.12) has exactly 2 nodes — one on each side of the battery.'},{q:'When are two elements in parallel?',options:['When they sit on the same unbranched path','When they carry the same current','When they connect between the same two nodes'],answer:2,why:'Parallel elements share both endpoints, so they have the same voltage. Sharing one current on an unbranched path describes series instead.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §1.1, printed pp. 4–10, Example 1.2.'),
noteSection('charge-current-voltage','1.2 · CHARGE, CURRENT, VOLTAGE','Count passing charge; mark a height difference.','Charge q is the fundamental quantity, measured in coulombs; the electron carries −1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. Current is the rate at which charge crosses a surface, i = dq/dt, with 1 A = 1 C/s. Voltage is the work per unit charge between two points, v = dw/dq, measured in volts — it is always a difference between two points, never a property of one point. Constant quantities are written as uppercase V and I (dc); time-varying quantities use lowercase v(t) and i(t).','Picture a chairlift. Voltage is the height gained between the bottom and top stations; current is the number of skiers per minute passing a gate. Nobody quotes the height “of a gate” — only the difference between two stations, and the resort’s altitude above sea level is a reference choice that cancels out of every difference. The analogy ends there: skiers return by slope, while charge needs a closed circuit.',String.raw`i=\frac{dq}{dt},\qquad v=\frac{dw}{dq}`,['One ampere is one coulomb per second; typical currents involve enormous numbers of elementary charges.','An arrow next to i and a +/− pair next to v are chosen references; a negative value simply means the actual direction or polarity is opposite.','Positive current is defined as the flow of positive charge, even though the moving carriers in metal wires are electrons.','The voltage across an element does not depend on which node you chose as the reference, nor on the value you assigned to it.'],{title:'Charge and current in a copper wire (Example 1.3)',steps:[['Compute the wire volume',String.raw`\mathrm{Vol}=L\pi r^2=(1)\pi(1\times10^{-3})^2=\pi\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m}^3`,'Length times cross-sectional area, with r = 10⁻³ m.'],['Count the carriers',String.raw`N=\mathrm{Vol}\times n=(\pi\times10^{-6})(10^{29})=\pi\times10^{23}`,'Volume times carrier density gives the number of electrons in the wire.'],['Multiply by the electron charge',String.raw`Q=Nq_e=(\pi\times10^{23})(-1.602\times10^{-19})\approx-50.33\times10^{3}\,\mathrm C`,'Each carrier contributes qₑ = −1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.'],['Convert drifting charge to current',String.raw`I=\frac{Q}{L}\,u=(-50.33\times10^{3})(19.9\times10^{-6})\approx-1\,\mathrm A`,'Charge per unit length times the average carrier speed gives charge per second.']],answer:'The metre of wire holds about −50.3 × 10³ C of mobile electron charge, drifting at I ≈ −1 A; the sign says the electron drift is opposite to conventional current.'},{q:'A steady 2 A flows through a wire for 3 s. How much charge passes a cross-section?',options:['0.67 C','1.5 C','6 C'],answer:2,why:'q = i·Δt = 2 × 3 = 6 C. Dividing or inverting confuses a rate with an amount.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §1.2, printed pp. 10–14, Example 1.3.'),
noteSection('iv-sources','1.3 · i–v CHARACTERISTICS & SOURCES','The source fixes one number; the load sets the other.','Every element owns an i–v characteristic — the plot of its current against its voltage. An ideal voltage source holds its terminal voltage at a prescribed value no matter how much current flows; its i–v plot is a vertical line. An ideal current source holds its current prescribed regardless of the voltage across it; its plot is a horizontal line. In each case the circuit connected to the source determines the quantity the source does not fix.','An ideal voltage source is an escalator: it adds the same height whether one rider or a crowd is on it, and the crowd size is decided by the riders. An ideal current source is a turnstile timed to admit exactly ten people a minute; the pressure building behind it is whatever the queue demands. Real devices sag under load, which is where the analogy ends.',String.raw`v=V_S\ \text{for any } i,\qquad i=I_S\ \text{for any } v`,['The + and − marks on a source show reference polarity; they do not claim the node voltages are positive or negative relative to ground.','A 12-V car battery barely notices its headlamps, but its voltage drops while the starter motor cranks — real sources are only nearly ideal.','An open circuit carries no current at any voltage, and a short circuit holds no voltage at any current; the book formalizes both in §1.6.','Dependent sources, drawn as diamonds, prescribe a voltage or current set by a signal elsewhere in the circuit.'],{title:'Headlamps on a 12-V battery (Check Your Understanding, p. 21)',steps:[['Let the source do its one job',String.raw`v=12\,\mathrm V\ \text{across each headlamp}`,'Both lamps connect between the same two nodes, so the ideal battery fixes 12 V across each.'],['Let each load choose its current',String.raw`i=\frac{p}{v}=\frac{50}{12}\approx4.17\,\mathrm A`,'A 50-W lamp at 12 V draws p/v; the source does not decide this.'],['Add the branch currents',String.raw`i_{\text{batt}}=4.17+4.17\approx8.33\,\mathrm A`,'The battery feeds both lamps through one node, so the two lamp currents add.'],['Compute the battery power',String.raw`p=vi=12\times8.33\approx100\,\mathrm W`,'Twelve volts times the total current — equivalently, 50 W + 50 W.']],answer:'Each headlamp draws 4.17 A, the battery supplies 8.33 A, and it provides 100 W.'},{q:'An ideal 12-V source is connected to a changing load. What stays fixed?',options:['The voltage across the load','The current through the load','The power delivered to the load'],answer:0,why:'An ideal voltage source prescribes voltage; the current and the power follow whatever the load demands.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §1.3, printed pp. 14–16; §1.4, printed p. 21, Check Your Understanding.'),
noteSection('power-psc','1.4 · POWER & SIGN CONVENTION','Read the sign of p = vi like a referee.','Power is the product of the voltage across an element and the current through it: p = vi, in watts. The passive sign convention draws the current reference arrow into the + voltage terminal; under it, p > 0 means the element absorbs energy (dissipating or storing it) and p < 0 means the element supplies energy. If the references are drawn oppositely, use p = −vi. Conservation of energy makes the powers of all elements sum to zero — a free audit of every circuit solution.','Think of each element as a bank account and power as a transfer rate. With the passive sign convention, positive p is money flowing in; a negative entry means the account funds the others. At the end of the day every transfer is someone else’s deposit, so all the accounts sum to zero. Power is not literally money, but the bookkeeping rule is exact.',String.raw`p=vi,\qquad \sum_{\text{all }k} p_k=0`,['On an i–v plot drawn with the passive sign convention, power is positive in the first and third quadrants and negative in the second and fourth.','A resistor always absorbs: its p can never be negative.','You do not need to foresee actual directions; assume them in accord with the convention and let a negative answer correct you.','Conservation of power is an independent check: supplied watts must equal absorbed watts.'],{title:'Audit a five-element circuit (Example 1.5)',steps:[['Write each supplier’s power with its sign',String.raw`p_A=-60\,\mathrm W,\qquad p_B=-15\,\mathrm W`,'A and B are measured against the passive pairing, so their products come out negative — they supply.'],['List the absorbers',String.raw`p_C=25\,\mathrm W,\quad p_D=30\,\mathrm W,\quad p_E=20\,\mathrm W`,'Positive products under the convention mean energy is consumed or stored.'],['Total each side of the ledger',String.raw`60+15=75\,\mathrm W\ \text{supplied},\qquad 25+30+20=75\,\mathrm W\ \text{absorbed}`,'Supplied and absorbed totals are computed independently of each other.'],['Close the books',String.raw`\sum_k p_k=-60-15+25+30+20=0`,'The zero sum confirms conservation of power.']],answer:'A and B supply 75 W in total; C, D and E absorb 75 W; the five powers sum to zero, so conservation holds.'},{q:'An element has v = 6 V with the current arrow entering the + terminal, and i = −2 A. The element is…',options:['absorbing 12 W','supplying 12 W','absorbing −12 W'],answer:1,why:'p = vi = 6 × (−2) = −12 W; negative absorbed power means the element supplies 12 W. Saying “absorbing −12 W” is equal in value but poor bookkeeping.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §1.4, printed pp. 17–21, Example 1.5.'),
noteSection('kirchhoff',"1.5 · KIRCHHOFF'S LAWS",'Balance every junction. Close every loop.','Kirchhoff’s current law says the signed sum of currents at any node — or any closed boundary — is zero, because charge does not accumulate. Kirchhoff’s voltage law says the signed sum of voltages around any closed loop is zero, because a charge returning to its starting point has recovered its original energy. Both laws are bookkeeping statements about the reference directions you chose, so the discipline lives in the signs, not in guessing actual directions first.','KCL is a road intersection: cars in must equal cars out, because no car park appears inside the junction. KVL is a circular hiking trail: the total climb must equal the total descent by the time you return to the trailhead. Signs are how you keep “in” apart from “out”, and “climb” apart from “descent”.',String.raw`\sum_{\text{node}} i_k=0,\qquad \sum_{\text{loop}} v_k=0`,['Drawing every current arrow out of a node is legal; a negative answer then means that current actually enters.','Applying KCL at every node produces one redundant equation — the reference-node balance adds nothing new.','KVL works around any closed path, even one that jumps across open space between two nodes.','KCL extends to a supernode: draw a closed boundary around several nodes and balance the currents crossing it.'],{title:'Stack thirty-one batteries (Example 1.9)',steps:[['See one loop',String.raw`\text{battery pack}\to\text{motor drive}\to\text{back to the pack}`,'The pack and the electric drive form a single closed path.'],['Walk the loop with KVL',String.raw`v_{\text{drive}}=v_{\text{batt},1}+v_{\text{batt},2}+\cdots+v_{\text{batt},31}`,'Around the loop, the rise the drive receives equals the sum of the battery rises.'],['Insert the nominal cell voltage',String.raw`v_{\text{drive}}=31\times12=372\,\mathrm V`,'Thirty-one 12-V batteries in series add directly.'],['Check the fully charged case',String.raw`31\times13=403\approx400\,\mathrm V`,'A charged lead-acid battery sits nearer 13 V, so the pack delivers about 400 V.']],answer:'The drive is nominally supplied by 372 V, and by closer to 400 V when the pack is fully charged.'},{q:'At one node, currents of 3 A and −5 A leave through two branches. With its arrow also drawn leaving, the third branch current is…',options:['−8 A','8 A','2 A'],answer:2,why:'Signed currents sum to zero: 3 + (−5) + i₃ = 0 gives i₃ = 2 A. The −5 A branch actually carries 5 A into the node.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §1.5, printed pp. 22–28, Example 1.9.'),
noteSection('resistance-ohm',"1.6 · RESISTANCE & OHM'S LAW",'Let v = iR be the resistor’s whole story.','Resistance R, in ohms, lumps an element’s opposition to charge flow into one number. Ohm’s law states that the voltage across an ideal resistor is proportional to the current through it: v = iR, drawn with the passive sign convention, so the resistor’s i–v plot is a straight line through the origin. The inverse G = 1/R is conductance, in siemens. A resistor dissipates p = i²R = v²/R, always positive, which is why every physical resistor carries a power rating.','A resistor is a gravel path. The harder you push (voltage), the more walkers per minute get through (current), and the path’s difficulty R sets the ratio. The push the walkers lose becomes heat in the gravel — i²R — which is why the path carries a load limit, the power rating. Real paths clog at rush hour, and real resistors stay linear only over a range.',String.raw`v=iR,\qquad p=i^2R=\frac{v^2}{R}`,['The i–v line of a resistor passes through the origin with slope 1/R; it never enters the power-supplying quadrants.','Conductance G = 1/R, in siemens, often tidies node equations: i = Gv.','Power grows with the square of voltage or current, so doubling the voltage quadruples the heat.','A short circuit (v = 0 for any i) and an open circuit (i = 0 for any v) are the limiting cases R → 0 and R → ∞.'],{title:'Respect a quarter-watt rating (Example 1.12)',steps:[['Start from the power law',String.raw`p=\frac{v^2}{R}\ \Rightarrow\ R=\frac{v^2}{p}`,'The battery prescribes the voltage, so solve the dissipation formula for R.'],['Set p equal to the rating',String.raw`R_{\min}=\frac{v^2}{0.25\,\mathrm W}`,'The smallest safe resistor dissipates exactly its rated 0.25 W.'],['Evaluate at 1.5 V',String.raw`R_{\min}=\frac{1.5^2}{0.25}=\frac{2.25}{0.25}=9\,\Omega`,'Square first, then divide.'],['Evaluate at 3 V',String.raw`R_{\min}=\frac{3^2}{0.25}=\frac{9}{0.25}=36\,\Omega`,'Doubling the voltage quadruples the required resistance.']],answer:'Use at least 9 Ω across 1.5 V and at least 36 Ω across 3 V — power is not linear in voltage.'},{q:'A 4 Ω resistor carries 3 A. How much power does it dissipate?',options:['12 W','48 W','36 W'],answer:2,why:'p = i²R = 9 × 4 = 36 W. Multiplying i × R gives the voltage across the resistor, not its power.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §1.6, printed pp. 29–37, Example 1.12.'),
noteSection('node-voltage','1.7 · NODE VOLTAGE METHOD','Pick a zero. Balance every other node.','The node voltage method turns KCL into a linear system. Choose one node as the reference (0 V), name the voltages of the remaining n − 1 nodes, and write each resistor current as a voltage difference divided by resistance — (start − end)/R in the direction of travel. Applying KCL at each unknown node gives n − 1 independent equations, and their solution yields every branch current through Ohm’s law.','Choose sea level, then read each neighbourhood’s altitude off a map. Water flow between neighbourhoods follows altitude differences, so you never have to measure flow directly. Node analysis assigns every node an “altitude” and lets the balance at each junction determine all of them at once.',String.raw`i_{a\to b}=\frac{v_a-v_b}{R}`,['A smart reference node touches the most elements or sources; the choice is free, but it changes how hard the algebra works.','The (start − end)/R pattern keeps every sign consistent with the current arrow you drew.','KCL at the reference node would only repeat the other equations, so n nodes give n − 1 independent equations.','A voltage source adjacent to the reference simply fixes a node voltage; one floating between two nodes calls for a supernode.'],{title:'Solve a two-node network (Example 1.13)',steps:[['Ground the bottom rail',String.raw`v_{\text{ref}}=0,\qquad \text{unknowns } v_1,\ v_2`,'There are no voltage sources and every node touches four elements, so any reference works.'],['Balance node 1',String.raw`10-\frac{v_1}{1}-\frac{v_1-v_2}{2}-\frac{v_1-v_2}{10}=0`,'mA with kΩ gives volts: the 10-mA source enters, three resistor currents leave, each written (start − end)/R.'],['Balance node 2',String.raw`\frac{v_1-v_2}{2}+\frac{v_1-v_2}{10}-\frac{v_2}{2}-50=0`,'The same two shared branches enter from node 1; R4 and the 50-mA source leave.'],['Collect and solve',String.raw`\begin{cases}1.6v_1-0.6v_2=10\\-0.6v_1+1.1v_2=-50\end{cases}\Rightarrow v_1=-13.57\,\mathrm V,\ v_2=-52.86\,\mathrm V`,'A 2 × 2 elimination; the determinant is 1.6 × 1.1 − 0.6 × 0.6 = 1.4.'],['Recover a branch current',String.raw`i_{R_3}=\frac{v_1-v_2}{10}=\frac{-13.57+52.86}{10}\approx3.93\,\mathrm{mA}`,'Both node voltages are negative, so R1’s current actually flows upward into node 1 — the sign, not a guess, tells you.']],answer:'v₁ ≈ −13.57 V and v₂ ≈ −52.86 V; both nodes sit below the reference, and the 10-kΩ branch carries ≈ 3.93 mA from node 1 toward node 2.'},{q:'In the node method, the current through R in the direction a → b is written as…',options:['(v_a + v_b)/R','(v_b − v_a)/R','(v_a − v_b)/R'],answer:2,why:'Start minus end, matching the arrow: current leaves a and enters b. Flipping the order flips the sign of every term the branch touches.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §1.7, printed pp. 38–47, Example 1.13.'),
noteSection('mesh-current','1.8 · MESH CURRENT METHOD','Give every mesh its own clockwise current.','The mesh current method assigns one circulating current to each mesh — this book always chooses clockwise — and writes KVL around each mesh. A resistor shared by two meshes has a drop R(i_this − i_neighbour). A branch current is the algebraic sum of the mesh currents sharing it. A current source bordering one mesh fixes that mesh current directly; one shared by two meshes links their difference and calls for a supermesh.','Picture a water park with adjacent lazy-river loops. Each loop keeps its own circulation, and a swimmer on the wall shared by two loops feels only the difference of the two currents. Mesh analysis names every loop’s circulation first and recovers the shared-wall flows afterwards.',String.raw`i_{\text{shared branch}}=i_1-i_2`,['Mesh currents are a bookkeeping device; only branch currents are physically measurable.','Keeping every mesh current clockwise makes each shared-branch drop a difference i_this − i_neighbour.','A current source on one mesh gives that mesh current directly.','A current source shared by two meshes supplies a difference equation; write KVL around the enclosing supermesh.'],{title:'Three meshes, one current source (Example 1.19)',steps:[['Count the equations',String.raw`n=3\ \text{meshes},\ m=1\ \text{source}\Rightarrow 2\ \text{KVL equations}`,'The current source borders only mesh 1, so i₁ is known immediately.'],['Take the free mesh current',String.raw`i_1=i_S=0.5\,\mathrm A`,'A source on a single mesh fixes that mesh current by inspection.'],['KVL around mesh 2',String.raw`-8(i_2-i_1)-6(i_2-i_3)+6=0\ \Rightarrow\ 14i_2-6i_3=10`,'The drops across R2 and R3 use the net branch currents; the source rises 6 V.'],['KVL around mesh 3',String.raw`-3(i_3-i_1)-4i_3-6(i_3-i_2)=0\ \Rightarrow\ -6i_2+13i_3=1.5`,'R3 is shared with mesh 2 and R1 with mesh 1; R4 belongs to mesh 3 alone.'],['Solve and check',String.raw`i_2\approx0.95\,\mathrm A,\quad i_3\approx0.55\,\mathrm A;\qquad 14(0.95)-6(0.55)=10\ \checkmark`,'Elimination on the 2 × 2 system, then substitution back into the mesh-2 equation.']],answer:'i₁ = 0.5 A, i₂ ≈ 0.95 A, i₃ ≈ 0.55 A in the clockwise convention; the exact values are 139/146 A and 81/146 A.'},{q:'A resistor is shared by meshes 1 and 2, both clockwise. The current through it, taken in mesh 1’s direction, is…',options:['i₁ − i₂','i₁ + i₂','i₂ alone'],answer:0,why:'The two mesh currents pass through the shared branch in opposite directions, so the branch current is their difference.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §1.8, printed pp. 48–55, Example 1.19.'),
noteSection('dependent-sources','1.9 · METHODS WITH DEPENDENT SOURCES','Keep the diamond. Add its constraint.','A dependent source is solved with the same node-voltage or mesh-current method as an independent source. The diamond source may introduce one extra unknown, but its controlling relationship supplies the matching constraint equation. Write KCL or KVL first, write the dependency second, substitute the constraint, and only then solve the reduced linear system.','A dependent source is a smart valve connected to a remote sensor. The valve still sits in the local pipe network like any other source, but its opening is tied to a measurement somewhere else. The circuit equations describe the local plumbing; the constraint describes the control wire.',String.raw`\text{circuit equations}+\text{source constraint}=\text{solvable system}`,['Keep the controlling voltage or current clearly labeled; it is part of the data, not decoration.','Example 1.20 uses node voltage and KCL with v₁ = 2v₃; Example 1.21 uses mesh current and KVL.','A dependent source can supply or absorb power; the diamond symbol does not decide the power sign.','Choose node or mesh analysis by equation count and source placement, exactly as before.'],{title:'Node voltage with a dependent source (Example 1.20)',steps:[['Choose ground and read the constraint',String.raw`v_1=2v_3,\qquad \text{unknowns }v_2,\ v_3`,'The bottom wire is the reference. The dependent voltage source determines v₁ once v₃ is known, so v₁ is not an independent unknown.'],['Write KCL at node 2',String.raw`\frac{v_1-v_2}{R_1}+i_S-\frac{v_2-v_3}{R_2}=0`,'Currents arriving from R1 and the source equal the current leaving through R2.'],['Write KCL at node 3',String.raw`\frac{v_2-v_3}{R_2}-\frac{v_3}{R_3}=0`,'Only R2 and R3 touch node 3; each current is written from start voltage to end voltage.'],['Substitute and collect',String.raw`\begin{cases}0.7v_2-0.9v_3=0.5\\0.5v_2-0.75v_3=0\end{cases}`,'Use v₁ = 2v₃ with iS = 0.5 A, R1 = 5 Ω, R2 = 2 Ω, and R3 = 4 Ω.'],['Solve, then recover the controlled node',String.raw`v_3=\frac{10}{3}=3.33\,\mathrm V,\quad v_2=5\,\mathrm V,\quad v_1=2v_3=6.67\,\mathrm V`,'The constraint is used again at the end to recover v₁ and audit the dependency.']],answer:'v₃ = 10/3 V ≈ 3.33 V, v₂ = 5 V, and v₁ = 20/3 V ≈ 6.67 V.'},{q:'What turns equations containing an unknown dependent-source value into a solvable system?',options:['Deleting the source','The source’s constraint equation','Assuming the diamond supplies power'],answer:1,why:'The controlling relationship adds the missing equation. Treat the source normally in KCL or KVL, then substitute its constraint.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §1.9, printed pp. 56–62, Example 1.20.')
],
2:[
noteSection('series-division','2.1 · SERIES RESISTORS & VOLTAGE DIVISION','One loop, one current, divided voltage.','Elements in series sit along a single current path, so one current crosses them all and KVL splits the branch voltage among them. The equivalent resistance is the sum, always larger than any individual resistor, and the voltage across each resistance is the same fraction of the total voltage as its share of the total resistance. The rule applies to any group of series resistances, not just discrete pairs, and it is the working principle behind divider sensors and the Wheatstone bridge.','Picture a staircase between two floors. Every traveller (the current) climbs every step, and the total height (the source voltage) is split among the steps in proportion to their risers (the resistances). This is only an analogy: no charge is used up on the way, but the proportional split of height is exactly the divider rule.',String.raw`R_{EQ}=R_1+R_2+\cdots,\qquad v_k=\frac{R_k}{R_{EQ}}\,v_S\qquad(\text{series})`,['Series means one shared current on an unbranched path; that is what makes the voltage ratio equal the resistance ratio.','R_EQ exceeds the largest individual resistance, giving a fast sanity bound on any series reduction.','The divider fraction applies to groups too: the voltage across R₁ + R₂ of a three-resistor chain is v_S·(R₁+R₂)/(R₁+R₂+R₃).','Signs follow the marked polarities: in Example 2.1 the source forces every drop v₁, v₂, v₃ positive.'],{title:'Voltage division in one loop (Example 2.1)',steps:[['One branch, one current',String.raw`\text{single loop}\Rightarrow\text{one }i\text{ through all three resistors}`,'Every element sits on the only current path, so voltage division applies directly.'],['Sum the series resistances',String.raw`R_{EQ}=10+6+8=24\,\Omega`,'Series resistances add; the result must exceed every individual value.'],['Take R₃’s share of the source',String.raw`v_3=v_S\frac{R_3}{R_{EQ}}=3\times\frac{8}{24}\,\mathrm{V}`,'The fraction of total voltage equals the fraction of total resistance.'],['Read the result and audit KVL',String.raw`v_3=1\,\mathrm{V},\qquad v_1+v_2+v_3=1.25+0.75+1=3\,\mathrm{V}`,'The three drops rebuild the 3-V source, each positive with the marked polarity.']],answer:'v₃ = 1 V. The 8-Ω resistor is one third of the 24-Ω chain, so it holds one third of the 3-V source. KVL audit: 1.25 + 0.75 + 1 = 3 V.'},{q:'A 12-V source drives R₁ = 2 kΩ in series with R₂ = 4 kΩ. What is v₂?',options:['4 V','8 V','12 V'],answer:1,why:'v₂ = 12 × 4/(2+4) = 8 V. In series the larger resistance takes the larger share of the voltage.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §2.1, printed pp. 80–87, Example 2.1.'),
noteSection('parallel-division','2.2 · PARALLEL RESISTORS & CURRENT DIVISION','Two nodes, one voltage, divided current.','Elements in parallel sit between the same two nodes, so one voltage lies across them all and KCL divides the entering current among them — in inverse proportion to resistance. The equivalent resistance is the reciprocal sum, always smaller than the smallest branch. For two resistors the current through one is the source current times the other resistance over the sum; for three or more, divide in proportion to conductance.','Picture a river splitting around an island. The water (the current) divides between the two channels in inverse proportion to how rocky (resistive) each is, then rejoins downstream. This is only an analogy: charge is not a fluid, but the inverse-ratio split and the recombination at the far node are exact.',String.raw`\frac{1}{R_{EQ}}=\sum_k\frac{1}{R_k},\qquad i_1=i_S\,\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}\qquad(\text{two in parallel})`,['Parallel means one shared voltage between the same two nodes; that is what makes the current ratio the inverse resistance ratio.','R_EQ is smaller than the smallest branch, giving a fast sanity bound on any parallel reduction.','The two-resistor form puts the other resistance on top: i₁ = i_S·R₂/(R₁+R₂) — the mirror of the divider’s own-resistance-on-top.','With three or more branches, divide by conductance: i_k = i_S·(1/R_k)·R_EQ, since 1/R_EQ = Σ(1/R_j).'],{title:'Current division among three branches (Example 2.3)',steps:[['Three resistors, one node pair',String.raw`\text{same two nodes}\Rightarrow\text{one }v\text{ across }R_1,R_2,R_3`,'R₁ = 10 Ω, R₂ = 2 Ω, R₃ = 20 Ω all sit between the same nodes as the 4-A source.'],['Sum the conductances',String.raw`\frac{1}{R_{EQ}}=\frac{1}{10}+\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{20}=0.65\,\mathrm{S}\Rightarrow R_{EQ}=1.538\,\Omega`,'The equivalent resistance, 20/13 Ω, is below the smallest branch — the bound checks.'],['Take R₁’s conductance share',String.raw`i_1=i_S\,\frac{1/R_1}{1/R_{EQ}}=4\times\frac{0.1}{0.65}=0.615\,\mathrm{A}`,'Each branch takes the fraction of the total current equal to its share of the total conductance.'],['Cross-check via R₂ ∥ R₃',String.raw`R_2\|R_3=\frac{2\times20}{22}=1.818\,\Omega,\qquad i_1=4\times\frac{1.818}{10+1.818}=0.615\,\mathrm{A}`,'Collapse two branches, then use the two-resistor form with the other resistance on top — same answer.']],answer:'i₁ ≈ 0.615 A. KCL audit: i₂ = 3.077 A and i₃ = 0.308 A rebuild the 4-A source, and i₂ = 5i₁, i₁ = 2i₃ because R₁ = 5R₂ and R₃ = 2R₁.'},{q:'A 6-A source feeds R₁ = 3 Ω in parallel with R₂ = 6 Ω. What is i₁?',options:['2 A','3 A','4 A'],answer:2,why:'Current favors the smaller resistance: i₁ = 6 × R₂/(R₁+R₂) = 6 × 6/9 = 4 A. The numerator is the other resistor.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §2.2, printed pp. 88–92, Example 2.3.'),
noteSection('equivalent-resistance','2.3 · EQUIVALENT RESISTANCE BETWEEN TWO NODES','Name two nodes. Crush what sits between them.','Equivalent resistance is not a property of a circuit but of a pair of nodes: apply a source across the pair and R_EQ = v_S/i_S is whatever the terminals report. Most resistive networks collapse by repeated series and parallel substitutions. When the drawing hides the topology, redraw it — count and letter the nodes, space them on a line, place each resistor between its two nodes, and collapse. When nothing is in series or parallel, the wye-delta transformation creates collapsible connections.','Equivalent resistance is like quoting one door-to-door travel time between two stations, whatever the metro map looks like inside. Replace the insides, keep the terminal behavior. This is only an analogy: travel times do not combine like resistances, but the idea — equivalence defined at the boundary — is exactly right.',String.raw`R_{EQ}=\frac{v_S}{i_S}\ \Big|_{\text{terminal pair}},\qquad R_1\|R_2=\frac{R_1R_2}{R_1+R_2}<\min(R_1,R_2)`,['Equivalence belongs to the terminal pair: Example 2.5’s one network gives 5 kΩ for a ↔ b but 8 kΩ for a ↔ c.','Redraw recipe: letter the nodes (terminals take the first and last letters), draw circles on a line, place every resistor between its two circles, then collapse.','Mental math: if R₂ = N·R₁, then R_EQ = R₂/(N+1) — equal resistors halve, 2:1 gives R₂/3, 3:1 gives R₂/4.','The 10:1 rule approximates a parallel pair by the smaller resistor with under 10% error; never chain the approximation through a network.'],{title:'Same network, two terminal pairs (Example 2.5)',steps:[['Choose the pair a ↔ b',String.raw`\text{two paths }a\to b:\ 6K\ \text{direct},\quad 12K+18K\ \text{via }c`,'Equivalent resistance is defined for two nodes, so the first move is naming them.'],['Series-collapse the path through c',String.raw`12K+18K=30K`,'One current would cross both resistors on that path, so they add.'],['Parallel-collapse the two paths',String.raw`R_{ab}=6K\|30K=\frac{6\times30}{36}K=5K`,'Both paths sit between the same nodes a and b. Shortcut check: 30K is 5 × 6K, so R_EQ = 30K/6 = 5K.'],['Re-ask for the pair a ↔ c',String.raw`R_{ac}=12K\|(6K+18K)=12K\|24K=\frac{12\times24}{36}K=8K`,'Same network, different terminal pair, different answer. (The b ↔ c pair gives 18K ∥ 18K = 9K.)']],answer:'R_ab = 5 kΩ and R_ac = 8 kΩ in the same three-resistor network — proof that equivalence is a property of two nodes, not of the circuit.'},{q:'R₁ = 4 kΩ in parallel with R₂ = 12 kΩ gives R_EQ = ?',options:['3 kΩ','8 kΩ','16 kΩ'],answer:0,why:'R_EQ = (4 × 12)/(4 + 12) = 3 kΩ — parallel always lands below the smaller branch. Shortcut: 12K is 3 × 4K, so R_EQ = 12K/4 = 3 kΩ.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §2.3, printed pp. 93–99, Example 2.5.'),
noteSection('superposition','2.4 · LINEAR NETWORKS & SUPERPOSITION','One source at a time. Add the pieces.','A linear network’s every voltage and current is the sum of independent contributions, one per independent source. Turn all the others off — a zeroed voltage source becomes a short-circuit, a zeroed current source an open-circuit — solve each single-source circuit, and add the components. Dependent sources are not independent: they stay active in every component circuit.','Think of mixing a recording: solo each instrument (one source on), capture its track (the component), then sum the tracks to hear the full mix. This is only an analogy: the tracks sum without interacting precisely because the elements are linear; with a nonlinear element the metaphor breaks.',String.raw`v=\sum_{k=1}^{N}v^{(k)},\qquad 0\text{-V source}\to\text{short},\quad 0\text{-A source}\to\text{open}`,['Linearity is superposition plus homogeneity; a network built only of linear elements — ideal sources, resistors, capacitors, inductors — is linear with no further proof.','Across the whole decomposition each independent source must be on exactly once — never twice, never never.','Dependent sources are never zeroed; they ride along in every component circuit with their controlling variables.','Superposition trades efficiency for insight: it usually costs more algebra than node or mesh analysis, but it shows what each source owns.'],{title:'Two sources, two components (Example 2.8)',steps:[['Name the target and count sources',String.raw`v_R=v_R^{(i)}+v_R^{(v)}`,'Two independent sources — i_B = 12 A and v_G = 12 V — so v_R across R = 0.23 Ω has exactly two components.'],['Zero the voltage source (short it)',String.raw`v_R^{(i)}=\frac{i_B}{1/R_B+1/R_G+1/R}=\frac{12}{1+3.333+4.348}=1.38\,\mathrm{V}`,'With v_G shorted, all three resistors sit in parallel (R_EQ = 0.115 Ω) across the current source; Ohm’s law gives the component.'],['Zero the current source (open it)',String.raw`v_R^{(v)}=v_G\,\frac{R_B\|R}{R_G+R_B\|R}=12\times\frac{0.187}{0.487}=4.61\,\mathrm{V}`,'Voltage division: R_B ∥ R = 0.187 Ω against R_G = 0.3 Ω.'],['Add the components',String.raw`v_R=1.38+4.61=5.99\,\mathrm{V}`,'Signs follow the same marked polarity in every component circuit.']],answer:'v_R ≈ 5.99 V = 1.38 V (from the 12-A source) + 4.61 V (from the 12-V source). A single direct KCL at the upper node confirms 5.99 V — superposition costs more here, but it prices each source’s contribution.'},{q:'When applying superposition, a dependent voltage source is…',options:['replaced by a short-circuit','left active in every component circuit','replaced by an open-circuit'],answer:1,why:'Only independent sources are zeroed. Dependent sources stay in every component circuit — zeroing one would delete its controlling relationship.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §2.4, printed pp. 100–104, Example 2.8.'),
noteSection('source-load','2.5 · THE SOURCE-LOAD PERSPECTIVE','Split at two terminals: source versus load.','Cut a circuit at two terminals: the part you care about is the load, and everything else is the source. The source’s entire influence on the load is one straight i–v line — the load line — running from the open-circuit voltage to the short-circuit current. The load brings its own i–v law, and the circuit’s operating point is where the two curves cross.','Think of a supply-and-demand diagram: the source offers one price-versus-quantity line, the load brings its own curve, and the market clears where they cross. This is only an analogy: nothing negotiates here — KVL and the element laws force the intersection.',String.raw`i=\frac{v_S-v}{R_1}\ (\text{load line}),\qquad v=i\,R_2\ (\text{load})\ \Rightarrow\ v=v_S\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}`,['The partition needs exactly two terminals; everything not in the load is, by default, the source.','The load line has slope −1/R₁ and intercepts v_OC on the voltage axis and i_SC on the current axis; every resistive load from ∞ down to 0 lands somewhere on it.','Thévenin and Norton sources with v_S = i_S·R₁ draw the identical load line — no load can tell them apart.','The graphical crossing keeps working when the load’s i–v law bends, which is exactly the §2.11 nonlinear payoff.'],{title:'The intersection is the divider (Equations 2.31–2.33 on Example 2.1’s circuit)',steps:[['Partition at R₃',String.raw`\text{source: }v_S=3\,\mathrm{V},\ R_1+R_2=16\,\Omega;\qquad \text{load: }R_3=8\,\Omega`,'Keep the 8-Ω resistor as the load; fold the source and the other two series resistors into the source network.'],['Write the source’s load line',String.raw`i=\frac{3-v}{16}\ \Rightarrow\ v_{OC}=3\,\mathrm{V},\quad i_{SC}=\frac{3}{16}=0.1875\,\mathrm{A}`,'KVL around the source loop; the intercepts are the open-circuit voltage and short-circuit current of the source alone.'],['Write the load’s law',String.raw`v=8\,i`,'Ohm’s law for R₃: a ray through the origin whose slope is 1/8 in the i–v plane.'],['Intersect the two curves',String.raw`8\,i=3-16\,i\ \Rightarrow\ i=0.125\,\mathrm{A},\qquad v=1\,\mathrm{V}`,'The crossing returns exactly the voltage-division result of Example 2.1.']],answer:'The intersection sits at i = 0.125 A, v = 1 V across the 8-Ω load — the same answer voltage division gave, obtained here by crossing two curves. The method survives nonlinear loads, where division formulas do not.'},{q:'A source network measures v_OC = 10 V and i_SC = 2 A. Its internal (Thévenin) resistance is…',options:['0.2 Ω','5 Ω','20 Ω'],answer:1,why:'R_T = v_OC/i_SC = 10/2 = 5 Ω. The two intercepts of the load line fix the whole source model.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §2.5, printed pp. 105–107, Equations 2.26–2.33.'),
noteSection('source-transformation','2.6 · SOURCE TRANSFORMATIONS','Swap series-voltage for parallel-current.','A Thévenin source — v_T in series with R_T — and a Norton source — i_N in parallel with R_N = R_T — are interchangeable at the same two terminals whenever v_T = i_N·R_T. Each swap moves the resistor between series and parallel company, creating combinations that did not exist before; chaining the swaps crushes a busy network toward a single equivalent source.','Think of converting units before combining quantities: you cannot add cups to grams, so you convert, combine, and convert back. This is only an analogy: unit conversions are conventions, while a source transformation is provably invisible to anything attached to the terminals.',String.raw`v_T=i_N R_T,\qquad R_T=R_N,\qquad i_{SC}=\frac{v_T}{R_T}`,['Transform only at a genuine terminal pair, and point the Norton arrow out of the terminal the Thévenin + sign faces.','Transformations pay off by creating series or parallel resistor combinations that were not there before.','Chained swaps work piecemeal: transform one subnetwork, simplify, transform again — Example 2.9 does this four times.','Dependent sources may live inside transformed networks, as long as the controlling variable survives the simplification.'],{title:'Four swaps to one Norton source (Example 2.9)',steps:[['Transform both Thévenin pairs',String.raw`v_1/R_1=0.5\,\mathrm{A}\ (\text{parallel }R_1),\qquad v_2/R_3=25\,\mathrm{mA}\ (\text{parallel }R_3)`,'Each series voltage source plus resistor becomes a current source in parallel with the same resistor; arrows follow the voltage polarities.'],['Merge the three current sources',String.raw`i_{eq}=0.5-0.5+0.025=25\,\mathrm{mA}\ \text{downward}`,'Parallel current sources add with direction; the 50-V and 0.5-A contributions cancel, leaving 25 mA.'],['Merge the three parallel resistors',String.raw`R_1\|R_2\|R_3=100\|100\|200=40\,\Omega`,'One node pair, three resistors: conductances add to 25 mS.'],['Transform back and finish',String.raw`v=0.025\times40=1\,\mathrm{V},\quad 40+160=200\,\Omega\ \Rightarrow\ i_N=\frac{1}{200}=5\,\mathrm{mA}`,'Norton → Thévenin puts the 40 Ω in series with R₄ = 160 Ω; one last swap yields the single Norton source seen by R_o.']],answer:'The entire network collapses to one Norton source seen by the load: i_N = 5 mA in parallel with R_N = 200 Ω.'},{q:'A 12-V source in series with 4 Ω transforms into…',options:['3 A in parallel with 4 Ω','12 A in parallel with 4 Ω','3 A in series with 4 Ω'],answer:0,why:'i_N = v_T/R_T = 12/4 = 3 A, and the resistor keeps its value but moves to parallel. The pair (3 A ∥ 4 Ω) draws the same load line as (12 V + 4 Ω).' },'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §2.6, printed pp. 108–110, Example 2.9.'),
noteSection('thevenin-norton','2.7 · THÉVENIN & NORTON EQUIVALENT NETWORKS','Any linear one-port: one source, one resistor.','Viewed from its terminals, any linear one-port network — however tangled inside — behaves as exactly one voltage source in series with one resistor, or one current source in parallel with the same resistor. Find v_T as the open-circuit voltage, i_N as the short-circuit current, and R_T by zeroing the independent sources, by attaching a test source, or as v_OC/i_SC.','Think of summarizing the whole power grid behind a wall outlet as “a stiff voltage with a small internal resistance”: nothing plugged in can feel the difference. This is only an analogy: real grids drift and saturate, while the theorem’s equivalence is exact for every load because the network is linear.',String.raw`v_T=v_{OC},\qquad i_N=i_{SC},\qquad R_T=R_N=\frac{v_{OC}}{i_{SC}}`,['v_T is the open-circuit voltage: with the load off, no current means no drop across R_T.','i_N is the short-circuit current: with a wire across the terminals, R_N carries nothing and the wire carries i_SC.','R_T by reduction: zero the independent sources (shorts and opens) and collapse what remains between the terminals.','With dependent sources, keep them on and either attach a test source (R_T = v_S/i_S) or compute R_T = v_OC/i_SC with all sources live.'],{title:'One KCL builds the whole equivalent (Example 2.13)',steps:[['Name the load and the source network',String.raw`\text{load}=R_3=6\,\Omega;\qquad \text{everything left of }a\text{–}b=\text{the one-port}`,'The theorem replaces the source network only; the load stays attached at the end.'],['R_T: zero both independent sources',String.raw`R_T=R_1\|R_2=4\|12=3\,\Omega`,'Voltage source → short, current source → open; the 4-Ω and 12-Ω resistors then share one node pair.'],['v_T: one KCL at node a',String.raw`\frac{v_a-24}{4}+\frac{v_a}{12}=3\ \Rightarrow\ v_a=27\,\mathrm{V}=v_{OC}`,'With the load removed, the 3-A source feeds the divider; superposition would give the same 27 V.'],['Reattach the load',String.raw`i=\frac{v_T}{R_T+R_3}=\frac{27}{3+6}=3\,\mathrm{A}`,'One series loop now replaces the entire original network.']],answer:'v_T = 27 V and R_T = 3 Ω, so the load current is i = 3 A through R₃. The matching Norton form is i_N = v_T/R_T = 9 A in parallel with 3 Ω.'},{q:'A one-port measures v_OC = 20 V and i_SC = 0.5 A. Its Thévenin pair is…',options:['v_T = 10 V, R_T = 40 Ω','v_T = 20 V, R_T = 40 Ω','v_T = 20 V, R_T = 10 Ω'],answer:1,why:'v_T = v_OC = 20 V and R_T = v_OC/i_SC = 20/0.5 = 40 Ω — two terminal measurements pin down the whole network.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §2.7, printed pp. 111–131, Example 2.13.'),
noteSection('max-power','2.8 · MAXIMUM POWER TRANSFER','Match the load to the source.','With the source frozen as its Thévenin pair (v_T, R_T), the load power P_L = v_T²R_L/(R_T+R_L)² peaks where dP_L/dR_L = 0, which collapses to R_L = R_T and delivers P_max = v_T²/(4R_T). Matching sends half the generated power into R_T itself — maximum transfer is not maximum efficiency — and any load pulls the terminal voltage below v_T by i·R_T, the loading effect.','Think of bicycle gears: too light a gear and your legs spin with no push; too heavy and you stall; the fastest ride matches the gear to your legs. This is only an analogy: the bicycle optimizes comfort, while here calculus maximizes the real product v·i at R_L = R_T.',String.raw`P_L=\frac{v_T^2\,R_L}{(R_T+R_L)^2},\qquad R_L=R_T\ \Rightarrow\ P_{max}=\frac{v_T^2}{4R_T}`,['The condition comes from calculus: differentiate P_L(R_L), set it to zero, and the algebra reduces to R_L = R_T.','At the match, v_L = v_T/2 and efficiency is exactly 50% — the source’s own R_T burns as much as the load.','The peak is broad: a 2:1 mismatch still delivers 8/9 of P_max, so near-matching is usually good enough.','Loading is the flip side: v_L = v_T − iR_T, so good voltage sources have tiny R_T and good current sources huge parallel r_S.'],{title:'Match the speaker to the amplifier (Example 2.17)',steps:[['Model the amplifier',String.raw`\text{amplifier}\approx(v_T,\ R_T=8\,\Omega)`,'Any linear source reduces to its Thévenin pair; only R_T matters for this comparison.'],['Unmatched 16-Ω speaker',String.raw`P_U=\frac{v_T^2\,R_U}{(R_T+R_U)^2}=v_T^2\,\frac{16}{24^2}=\frac{v_T^2}{36}`,'Voltage division hands the load 2/3 of v_T; power follows as v²/R.'],['Matched 8-Ω speaker',String.raw`P_M=\frac{v_T^2\,R_M}{(R_T+R_M)^2}=v_T^2\,\frac{8}{16^2}=\frac{v_T^2}{32}`,'At R_L = R_T the load takes v_T/2 and the most power the source can give.'],['Compare',String.raw`\frac{P_M}{P_U}=\frac{36}{32}=1.125\ \Rightarrow\ +12.5\%`,'Matching buys 12.5% more power at the speaker from the same amplifier.']],answer:'Matching the speaker (8 Ω to the amplifier’s 8 Ω) raises delivered power by 12.5%, from v_T²/36 to v_T²/32 = v_T²/(4R_T) — the amplifier’s ceiling.'},{q:'A source has v_T = 24 V and R_T = 6 Ω. The maximum power it can deliver is…',options:['24 W','48 W','96 W'],answer:0,why:'P_max = v_T²/(4R_T) = 576/24 = 24 W, delivered when R_L = 6 Ω; another 24 W simultaneously heats the source itself.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §2.8, printed pp. 132–134, Example 2.17.'),
noteSection('practical-sources-meters','2.9–2.10 · PRACTICAL SOURCES & METERS','Real sources sag. Real meters disturb.','Practical sources carry internal resistance: a small series r_S for a voltage source (Thévenin model), a large parallel r_S for a current source (Norton model), which is why outputs droop under load. Meters are loads too — an ammeter inserts a small series resistance, a voltmeter a large parallel one, a wattmeter both — and an ohmmeter faithfully reports whatever parallel combination it sees across its probes.','Think of measuring tire pressure: the gauge must release a little air to read it, so the act of measuring slightly drops the pressure. This is only an analogy: a good voltmeter leaks microamps rather than air, but the lesson is identical — know your instrument’s appetite.',String.raw`v_o=v_S\,\frac{R_o}{r_S+R_o}\ (\text{voltage source}),\qquad i_o=i_S\,\frac{r_S}{r_S+R_o}\ (\text{current source})`,['Practical voltage source = ideal v_S in series with small r_S; the load voltage is v_S·R_o/(r_S+R_o), which sags when R_o approaches r_S.','Practical current source = ideal i_S in parallel with large r_S; the load current is i_S·r_S/(r_S+R_o), which sags when R_o approaches r_S.','Meter rules: ammeter in series with r_m ≪ branch resistance; voltmeter in parallel with r_m ≫ node-pair resistance.','An ohmmeter measures the parallel combination it faces — disconnect one end of the element, and never bridge it with your fingers.'],{title:'Unmask a voltmeter’s resistance (Example 2.18)',steps:[['Model the meter honestly',String.raw`r_m\ \text{in parallel with}\ R_2`,'The practical voltmeter loads the very divider it measures.'],['Divide with the meter attached',String.raw`v_2=v_S\,\frac{R_2\|r_m}{R_1+R_2\|r_m}`,'Voltage division on the parallel combination gives the measured value, not the ideal one.'],['Spot the clean condition',String.raw`R_1=R_2=R:\quad \frac{v_2}{v_S}=\frac{r_m}{R+2r_m}=\frac{1}{3}\ \Longleftrightarrow\ r_m=R`,'With equal divider resistors, a reading of exactly v_S/3 means the meter resistance equals each of them.'],['Read the table',String.raw`v_2=\frac{5.0}{3}=1.67\,\mathrm{V}\ \text{at}\ R_1=R_2=10\,\mathrm{M}\Omega\ \Rightarrow\ r_m=10\,\mathrm{M}\Omega`,'Table 2.1 shows the 1.67-V reading at the 10-MΩ pair — a value typical of handheld multimeters.']],answer:'r_m = 10 MΩ, betrayed by the v₂ = v_S/3 = 1.67 V reading when R₁ = R₂ = 10 MΩ. Row-by-row estimates scatter from 1.25 MΩ to 10.06 MΩ because the formula is error-sensitive when R₂ ≪ r_m.'},{q:'To measure the current through a 100-Ω branch, an ammeter’s internal resistance should be…',options:['about 100 Ω','as small as possible','as large as possible'],answer:1,why:'The ammeter goes in series, so its resistance adds to the branch. Only r_m ≪ 100 Ω leaves the current — and the circuit — essentially unchanged.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §§2.9–2.10, printed pp. 135–140, Example 2.18.'),
noteSection('nonlinear-loads','2.11 · NONLINEAR LOADS & LOAD-LINE ANALYSIS','When the load bends, draw the line.','Diodes, transistors, and many sensors have curved i–v characteristics, so closed-form answers usually do not exist. The chapter’s toolkit still applies: reduce everything linear around the nonlinear element to its Thévenin source, draw that source’s straight load line over the device curve, and read the intersection — or attack the resulting transcendental equation by numerical iteration.','Think of tuning a string against a reference tone: there is no formula to consult mid-performance, so you adjust until the two sounds coincide. This is only an analogy: the intersection here is exact — it satisfies KVL and the device law at the same time.',String.raw`v_T=v_x+i_x R_T\ (\text{load line}),\qquad i_x=f(v_x)\ (\text{device})\ \Rightarrow\ (v_x,i_x)\ \text{at the crossing}`,['One nonlinear load inside a linear network always reduces to load line versus device curve — the network’s interior complexity collapses into v_T and R_T.','The line runs from v_T on the voltage axis to v_T/R_T on the current axis; the device curve is measured or modeled separately.','Transcendental cases such as the exponential diode need iteration: guess v_x, compare both sides, re-bracket, repeat (Problem 2.93 formalizes the loop).','The graphical method doubles as an experimental procedure: measured device curves drop straight onto the same axes.'],{title:'Square-law load, solved exactly (Check Your Understanding, p. 144)',steps:[['Crush the linear side',String.raw`\text{load line: }15=v_x+30\,i_x`,'The generator of Example 2.19 is already a Thévenin source: 15 V and 30 Ω, with intercepts at 15 V and 0.5 A.'],['Write the device law',String.raw`v_x=15\,i_x^2\qquad(\beta=15)`,'The square-law load’s voltage grows with the square of its current.'],['Substitute into the line',String.raw`15=15\,i_x^2+30\,i_x\ \Rightarrow\ i_x^2+2i_x-1=0`,'One nonlinear equation in one unknown; this one keeps clean roots.'],['Keep the physical root',String.raw`i_x=\sqrt{2}-1=0.414\,\mathrm{A},\qquad v_x=15\,i_x^2=2.57\,\mathrm{V}`,'The negative root is rejected by the generator’s polarity; v_x = 15 − 30i_x confirms the load line.']],answer:'i_x = √2 − 1 ≈ 0.414 A and v_x ≈ 2.57 V, found analytically here — the same point appears graphically where the load line crosses the square-law curve. The exponential-load variant needs iteration and gives i_x ≈ 0.04 A.'},{q:'Load-line analysis solves a nonlinear circuit by…',options:['linearizing the device about zero volts','crossing the source’s load line with the device’s i–v curve','zeroing all sources but one'],answer:1,why:'KVL forces the operating point onto the source’s straight line; the device law forces it onto the curve. Their intersection satisfies both — no approximation involved.'},'Rizzoni & Kearns (2022), §2.11, printed pp. 141–145, Example 2.19 and its Check Your Understanding; chapter problems through p. 162.')
]};

const ELEC275_CHAPTER_ENHANCEMENTS={
 networks:{objective:'Identify nodes, branches, loops, meshes, and the source–load boundary even after a circuit is redrawn.',trap:'Do not count dots or corners. Count uninterrupted ideal-wire regions, and preserve which element sits between which pair of nodes.',coverage:[['Source and load','The load is the part you care about; the source is everything else. Power often flows source → load, but that is not guaranteed.'],['Connectivity, not geometry','Stretching, rotating, or redrawing an ideal wire does not change the circuit. The node-to-node connections are the circuit’s real map.'],['Series and parallel preview','Same unbranched path means the same branch current. Same two endpoint nodes means the same voltage. Full reduction rules begin in Chapter 2.']]},
 'charge-current-voltage':{objective:'Apply the definitions of charge, current, and voltage with correct units, references, and signs.',trap:'A current arrow and a voltage polarity are reference choices, not predictions. A negative answer corrects the assumed direction; it is not a failed solution.',coverage:[['Rate and accumulated charge','i = dq/dt, so a known current can be integrated over time to recover charge.'],['Voltage is a difference','Voltage is work per unit charge between two points. Changing the zero reference changes node labels, not any element voltage.'],['Conventional current','Positive current follows positive charge. In metals, electrons drift in the opposite direction.']]},
 'iv-sources':{objective:'Read i–v characteristics and distinguish ideal independent sources from controlled sources.',trap:'The +/− marks on a source define the measured voltage polarity. They do not declare either terminal positive relative to ground.',coverage:[['Ideal voltage source','Voltage stays prescribed; the connected circuit determines current. Its i–v characteristic is a vertical line.'],['Ideal current source','Current stays prescribed; the connected circuit determines voltage. Its i–v characteristic is a horizontal line.'],['Controlled sources','Diamond symbols model amplification: voltage- or current-controlled voltage or current sources. Section 1.9 shows how the constraint enters the equations.']]},
 'power-psc':{objective:'Use the passive sign convention to classify absorbed and supplied power and audit conservation.',trap:'p = vi applies directly only when the current reference enters the marked + terminal. If it enters the − terminal, use p = −vi.',coverage:[['Reference-pair test','Inspect the current arrow and voltage polarity before multiplying. The pairing decides the formula.'],['Absorb versus supply','Under PSC, positive p is absorbed; negative p means the element supplies |p|.'],['Power balance','Sum signed powers over every element. A correct circuit solution gives zero.']]},
 kirchhoff:{objective:'Apply KCL to boundaries and KVL to closed paths using one consistent sign rule.',trap:'Do not write every possible node or loop equation. One node equation is redundant, and dependent loop equations add algebra without information.',coverage:[['KCL · charge','Choose entering-positive or leaving-positive and keep it for the whole node equation. KCL also works on a supernode boundary.'],['KVL · energy','Choose a traversal direction. Crossing − to + is a rise; + to − is a drop. Return to the start and the signed changes sum to zero.'],['Independent equations','Use as many independent equations as unknowns, then check unused nodes or loops afterward.']]},
 'resistance-ohm':{objective:'Use Ohm’s law, resistance geometry, conductance, ratings, and practical resistor behavior safely.',trap:'Ohm’s law is a linear model over a valid operating range, not a universal claim that every device is a resistor.',coverage:[['Material and geometry','R = ρℓ/A: a longer conductor resists more, a larger cross-section resists less, and conductivity is σ = 1/ρ.'],['Open, short, and nonlinearity','A short holds v = 0; an open holds i = 0. Lamps and diodes can be nonlinear or only piecewise linear.'],['Real resistor language','Color bands encode nominal value and tolerance; E-series values standardize parts. 4K7 means 4.7 kΩ.'],['Sensors and controls','Photoresistors and thermistors turn light or temperature into resistance; a potentiometer turns a wiper position into a voltage-divider output.'],['Thermal reality','p = i²R = v²/R becomes heat. Check the power rating and worst-case tolerance, not just the nominal resistance.']]},
 'node-voltage':{objective:'Apply the node-voltage algorithm, including known source nodes and floating-source supernodes.',trap:'Do not write a resistor current as node voltage divided by R unless the other end is ground. In general it is (start − end)/R.',coverage:[['Base algorithm','Choose a reference, label unknown node voltages, write every resistor current from node differences, apply KCL, then solve.'],['Voltage source to known node','A source beside ground or another known node fixes the adjacent node voltage and reduces the unknown count.'],['Floating voltage source','Enclose both source terminals in a supernode, write one KCL equation for the boundary, then add the source-voltage relation.']]},
 'mesh-current':{objective:'Apply the mesh-current algorithm, including fixed mesh currents and shared-source supermeshes.',trap:'A mesh current is not automatically a branch current. On a shared branch, combine the adjacent mesh currents algebraically.',coverage:[['Base algorithm','Assign every mesh the same circulation direction, write KVL, and use R(i_this − i_neighbour) on shared resistors.'],['Source on one mesh','That current source fixes the mesh current directly, reducing the number of KVL equations.'],['Source shared by two meshes','Write the source difference equation, exclude its branch from KVL, and walk the outer supermesh instead.']]},
 'dependent-sources':{objective:'Solve node or mesh systems containing dependent sources by adding and using the controlling constraint.',trap:'Do not replace the diamond source with a guessed number. Its value remains tied to the labeled controlling voltage or current.',coverage:[['Write the circuit equations first','Treat the dependent source like its independent counterpart while writing KCL or KVL.'],['Add the control law','Write the diamond’s voltage/current relationship as an explicit equation, with the controlling variable defined.'],['Substitute, solve, verify','Eliminate the source variable when convenient, solve the reduced system, then confirm the final values still satisfy the constraint.']]},
 'series-division':{outcomeLabel:'TEXTBOOK OUTCOME 1 OF 9 · SHARED WITH CARD 02',objective:'Apply voltage and current division to calculate unknown voltages and currents in simple series, parallel, and series-parallel circuits — the textbook’s objective 1, which this card shares with the parallel-division card.',trap:'Voltage division needs one shared current. Tap the middle node with a third branch (a loaded divider) and the resistors are no longer purely in series — the unloaded divider formula then overstates the tap voltage.',coverage:[['The ratio needs no source value','Because one current crosses the whole branch, the ratio of any two voltage drops equals the ratio of the resistances; v_S cancels out of every ratio.'],['Groups divide too','The rule applies to any two series groups, not just discrete resistors: the share of R₁ + R₂ in a three-resistor chain is (R₁+R₂)/(R₁+R₂+R₃) of the total voltage.'],['The Wheatstone bridge is two dividers','Example 2.2 runs two dividers off one source: v_ab = v_S[R₂/(R₁+R₂) − R_x/(R₃+R_x)]. With R₁ = R₂ = R₃ = 1 kΩ, v_S = 12 V, v_ab = 12 mV gives R_x ≈ 996 Ω; balance (v_ab = 0) requires R₁R_x = R₂R₃, not four equal resistors.'],['Sensors are dividers at work','The throttle-position sensor and strain-gauge bridges (printed pp. 84–88) turn displacement into ΔR, then read it as a divider or bridge output — the 500-N beam gauge yields 62.6 mV full scale, a 0.125 mV/N calibration.']]},
 'parallel-division':{outcomeLabel:'TEXTBOOK OUTCOME 1 OF 9 · SHARED WITH CARD 01',objective:'Apply voltage and current division to calculate unknown voltages and currents in simple series, parallel, and series-parallel circuits — the textbook’s objective 1, which this card shares with the series-division card.',trap:'The two-resistor shortcut puts the other resistance in the numerator: i₁ = i_S·R₂/(R₁+R₂). Putting R₁ on top silently swaps the branch currents — the larger resistor must receive the smaller share.',coverage:[['Why the ratio inverts','One voltage across both branches means i_k = v/R_k, so currents split in proportion to conductance 1/R_k; resistance ratios flip upside down.'],['Three or more branches','Use conductances: i_k = i_S·(1/R_k)/(Σ 1/R_j) = i_S·R_EQ/R_k. The two-branch “other over sum” form is just the two-resistor special case.'],['Groups divide too','Current division applies to parallel groups, not only discrete resistors: the combined current through R₁ ∥ R₂ versus R₃ splits in the ratio R₃ to (R₁∥R₂) (Equation 2.6).'],['Series-parallel mixes','Real circuits blend both rules — Example 2.4 collapses R₂ ∥ R₃ first, then applies voltage division to the surviving series loop.']]},
 'equivalent-resistance':{objective:'Correctly redraw a resistive network, as necessary, and compute the equivalent resistance between two nodes — the textbook’s objective 2.',trap:'Do not carry R_EQ from one terminal pair to another. The same network gives 5 kΩ between a and b but 8 kΩ between a and c — equivalence belongs to the chosen pair of nodes.',coverage:[['The redraw algorithm','Count and letter the nodes (terminals take the first and last letters), draw the nodes as circles on a straight line, place every resistor between its two circles, then collapse series and parallel pairs until one resistance remains (Example 2.6 reduces A ↔ E to exactly 12 kΩ in five collapses).'],['Mental math for parallel pairs','If R₂ = N·R₁, then R_EQ = R₂/(N+1): equal resistors halve, 2:1 gives R₂/3, 3:1 gives R₂/4. Beyond about 10:1 the smaller resistor alone is within 10% — fine for inspection, dangerous if chained.'],['The wye-delta escape hatch','A loaded bridge leaves no series or parallel pair anywhere. The Y-Δ transformation (Equations 2.11–2.22) swaps a three-terminal delta for a wye attached to exactly the same nodes A, B, C, manufacturing collapsible connections.']]},
 'superposition':{objective:'Apply the principle of superposition to linear circuits containing independent and dependent sources — the textbook’s objective 3.',trap:'Power is not linear, so never add component powers. Sum component voltages or currents first, then square or multiply the total to get power.',coverage:[['What linearity buys','Superposition (outputs add) plus homogeneity (scaling the source scales its contribution). Any network built only of linear elements — ideal sources, resistors, capacitors, inductors — is linear without further testing.'],['Zeroing rules','A zeroed voltage source becomes a short-circuit; a zeroed current source becomes an open-circuit (Figure 2.19). Every independent source must be on exactly once across the decomposition.'],['When it earns its keep','Superposition shines when sources differ in kind and when you must know how much of an answer each source owns. For a single numeric answer, node or mesh analysis is usually faster — as Example 2.8’s own comment admits.']]},
 'source-load':{objective:'Apply the source-load perspective to find graphical solutions to circuit problems — the textbook’s objective 4.',trap:'The load line belongs to the source alone. Redrawing it after attaching the load, or mixing the load’s slope 1/R₂ with the source’s slope −1/R₁, double-counts the load; the solution is the crossing of two separate curves.',coverage:[['Source ≠ ideal source','The “source” here is a generalized one-port that may contain many ideal sources and resistors; ideal sources are merely its ingredients. The book always says voltage source or current source for the ideal kind.'],['Reading the load line','Slope −1/R_T, voltage-axis intercept v_OC, current-axis intercept i_SC. Sliding the load from ∞ down to 0 walks the operating point from one axis to the other.'],['Why the two source forms agree','A Thévenin source (v_S, R₁) and a Norton source (i_S, R₁) with v_S = i_S·R₁ share the same load line, so nothing outside the terminals can distinguish them — the seed of source transformations and of the §2.7 theorems.']]},
 'source-transformation':{objective:'Apply source transformations to simplify and solve linear circuits containing independent and dependent sources — the textbook’s objective 5.',trap:'Never transform a source through the load you are solving for, and never let a transformation swallow the controlling variable of a dependent source — that label must survive in the simplified circuit.',coverage:[['Why it is legitimate','Both forms draw the same load line: v_OC = v_T = i_N R_N and i_SC = v_T/R_T = i_N. Identical i–v at the terminals means identical behavior for any conceivable load.'],['What it buys','Each swap changes the local topology: voltage-source form puts the resistor in series (ready to add with series neighbors), current-source form puts it in parallel (ready to merge). Example 2.9 chains four swaps into one 5-mA ∥ 200-Ω Norton source.'],['Direction discipline','The Norton arrow points toward the terminal that the Thévenin + sign faces. Reversing it flips the sign of every downstream result.']]},
 'thevenin-norton':{objective:'Determine Thévenin and Norton equivalent circuits for networks containing linear resistors and independent and dependent sources — the textbook’s objective 6.',trap:'When zeroing sources to find R_T, zero only the independent ones. A dependent source left active can make R_T surprising — even negative — and that is real; a dependent source wrongly zeroed simply gives a wrong number.',coverage:[['Three routes to R_T','No dependent sources: zero the independents and collapse series/parallel. Topology that refuses to collapse: attach a test source and compute R_T = v_S/i_S. Dependent sources with at least one independent source: R_T = v_OC/i_SC measured with everything live.'],['The 8-kΩ, 1-V, 0.125-mA triad','Examples 2.11, 2.14, and 2.16 attack the same dependent-source network three independent ways and land on v_T = i_N·R_T = 1 V exactly — the theorem’s internal consistency made visible.'],['Measured equivalents','Real instruments are characterized the same way: measured 6.5 V open-circuit and 3.25 mA short-circuit suggest R_T ≈ 2.0 kΩ, refined to 1.975 kΩ once the 25-Ω ammeter resistance is honored (printed pp. 128–131).'],['Boundary cases','v_T = 0 leaves a plain resistor R_T; v_T = R_T = 0 is a short-circuit; i_N = 0 with infinite R_T is an open-circuit.']]},
 'max-power':{objective:'Use equivalent-circuit ideas to compute the maximum power transfer between a source and a load — the textbook’s objective 7.',trap:'Maximum power is not maximum efficiency. At R_L = R_T the efficiency is exactly 50%. Power distribution runs unmatched (R_L ≫ R_T) for efficiency; matching matters for small-signal and measurement links.',coverage:[['Where the theorem comes from','P_L = i²R_L with i = v_T/(R_T+R_L); setting dP_L/dR_L = 0 collapses to R_L = R_T (Equations 2.44–2.50). The peak is broad — a 2:1 mismatch still delivers 8/9 of P_max.'],['Loading is the flip side','Any load pulls v_L = v_T − iR_T below the open-circuit value. Good voltage sources keep R_T tiny; good current sources keep their parallel r_S huge.'],['Worked anchors','A 30-V source with 1.2 Ω internal resistance holds its droop under 2% only for R_L ≥ 58.8 Ω; a 200-mA source with 12 kΩ in parallel loses 1.64% of its current into a 200-Ω load (section Check Your Understanding).']]},
 'practical-sources-meters':{objective:'Understand the impact of internal resistance in practical models of voltage and current sources as well as of voltmeters, ammeters, and wattmeters — the textbook’s objective 8.',trap:'An ohmmeter measures whatever sits between its probes. Leave the resistor in its network — or clamp both ends with your fingers — and you read the parallel combination, always smaller than the truth.',coverage:[['Why sources sag','A car battery’s voltage visibly drops while starting the engine: the starter pulls hundreds of amps through the battery’s internal r_S. Even a 12-V, 450-A-h battery behaves as (v_S, r_S), not an ideal source.'],['Meter placement rules','Ammeter in series with the element, r_m ≪ branch resistance. Voltmeter in parallel with the element, r_m ≫ node-pair resistance. Break either rule and the meter rewrites the quantity it reads.'],['The catch-22, dissolved','Attaching a meter changes the circuit — but with a known r_m the disturbance is computable (Equations 2.53–2.57), so the true value can be recovered from the disturbed one.'],['The wattmeter inherits both','A wattmeter is an ammeter plus a voltmeter measuring one element simultaneously; it carries both instruments’ internal-resistance errors at once.']]},
 'nonlinear-loads':{objective:'Use the concept of equivalent circuits to determine voltage, current, and power for nonlinear loads by using load-line analysis and analytical methods — the textbook’s objective 9.',trap:'Superposition and equivalent-resistance shortcuts apply only to the linear part of the circuit. Never “turn off” a nonlinear element or split its response into per-source components — linearity is what made those moves legal.',coverage:[['The diode system','With a diode load the pair is v_T = v_x + i_xR_T and i_x ≈ I₀e^(v_x/V_th) — transcendental, so iterate: guess v_x, evaluate both sides, bracket the crossing, repeat (Problem 2.93 turns this into a flowchart).'],['Example 2.19 graphically','R_T = 30 Ω and v_T = 15 V: the line runs from 15 V on the voltage axis to 0.5 A on the current axis, and where it crosses the measured device curve you read off v_x, i_x, and the dissipated power P_x = v_x·i_x.'],['Why the method matters','This is the daily workflow for diodes, transistors, thermistors, and motors: measure or model the device curve, reduce everything else to two numbers, intersect.']]}
};
for(const section of ELEC275_CHAPTER_NOTES[1])Object.assign(section,ELEC275_CHAPTER_ENHANCEMENTS[section.id]);
for(const section of ELEC275_CHAPTER_NOTES[2])Object.assign(section,ELEC275_CHAPTER_ENHANCEMENTS[section.id]);
ELEC275_CHAPTER_NOTES[1].find(section=>section.id==='power-psc').points[1]='With voltage and current paired by the passive sign convention, a resistor has p = i²R = v²/R ≥ 0 and absorbs power.';
ELEC275_CHAPTER_NOTES[1].find(section=>section.id==='kirchhoff').points[2]='KVL works around any closed path identified by its sequence of nodes; the path does not need to be a mesh.';
ELEC275_CHAPTER_NOTES[1].find(section=>section.id==='node-voltage').summary='The node-voltage method turns KCL into a linear system. Choose one node as the reference (0 V), name the remaining unknown node voltages, and write each resistor current as (start − end)/R. Apply KCL at ordinary nodes and supernodes, add any voltage-source constraint equations, solve, and recover branch currents with Ohm’s law.';
